From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patrick Quinn <patrick.jm.quinn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:26:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: libipa: agc_mean_luminance: Don't wind up the exposure
 in the dark

When the image is black the lower bound constraints divide the target by
a mean close to zero, and the exposure value grows by up to 10^8. The
exposure time and analogue gain clamp it, the rest shows as a huge
digital gain, but the filtered exposure value keeps it: when the scene
brightens it only shrinks by a fifth per frame, so the image stays
overexposed for tens of statistics frames.

Clamp the filtered exposure value to what the exposure time and
analogue gain deliver.

Signed-off-by: Patrick Quinn <patrick.jm.quinn@gmail.com>
---
 src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp b/src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp
index 0c5ad469..9f68206a 100644
--- a/src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp
@@ -797,7 +797,20 @@ AgcMeanLuminance::calculateNewEv(const Params &params)
 	newExposureValue = filterExposure(newExposureValue);
 
 	frameCount_++;
-	return { exposureModeHelper.splitExposure(newExposureValue), yTarget };
+
+	ExposureModeHelper::Result result = exposureModeHelper.splitExposure(newExposureValue);
+
+	/*
+	 * In the dark the gain to the target has no bound. Don't let the
+	 * filtered exposure value wind up beyond what the exposure time and
+	 * analogue gain deliver, or it takes many frames to come back down when
+	 * the scene brightens.
+	 */
+	filteredExposure_ = std::min<utils::Duration>(filteredExposure_,
+						      result.exposureTime * result.analogueGain *
+						      result.quantizationGain);
+
+	return { result, yTarget };
 }
 
 } /* namespace ipa */
